<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Inowroclaw</h1><p class="st-meta">1267 CE – 1364 CE (approx.) · capital Inowroclaw</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kuyavian Piast duchy on the Teutonic border, ruled by Ziemomysl and his sons until Casimir the Great absorbed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the division of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-kuyavia">Duchy of Kuyavia</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-i-of-kuyavia">Casimir I</a>'s death in 1267, the northern lands around Inowroclaw went to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziemomysl-of-inowroclaw">Ziemomysl</a>. He relied on support from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, which caused revolts among his own nobles.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Inowroclaw was a salt-producing land near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, and its towns grew under German law. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziemomysl-of-inowroclaw">Ziemomysl</a> died in 1287 his sons Leszek, Przemysl and Casimir shared and exchanged its parts, including Gniewkowo. The duchy was devastated during the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> in the 1320s and 1330s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> gradually bought out the local dukes. The last claimant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-the-white">Wladyslaw the White of Gniewkowo</a>, gave up his rights and became a monk in the 1360s, and around 1364 Inowroclaw was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1025-1385).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-inowroclaw.jpg" alt="Duchy of Inowroclaw" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: Zuber &amp; MariuszR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polska_za_W_Lokietka_(1275-1300).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1267 CE – 1364 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Inowroclaw</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ziemomysl, Przemysl of Inowroclaw, Wladyslaw the White</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Inowroclaw</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1316&amp;focus=duchy-of-inowroclaw" aria-label="Duchy of Inowroclaw on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5089 358 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Inowroclaw lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5409 614L5409 614M5409 614L5457 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5409 614h0M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5409 614h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5409 614h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1316&amp;focus=duchy-of-inowroclaw">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
